…men who should consider the land, and report back by what way we ought to go up, and to which cities we should proceed. And the proposal pleased me: I sent from among you twelve men, one from each tribe. When they had gone forth and had climbed into the mountains, they came as far as the Valley of the Cluster, and having examined the land, taking from its fruits to show its fertility, they brought them to us and said: The land which the Lord our God is about to give us is good. But you were unwilling to go up; rather, being unbelieving toward the word of our God, you murmured in your tents and said: The Lord hates us; and therefore he brought us out of the land of Egypt, to deliver us into the hand of the Amorite and destroy us. Where shall we go up? The messengers terrified our heart, saying: The people are very numerous, and taller than we; the cities are great and fortified up to the sky; we saw the sons of Anak there. And I said to you: Do not fear, nor be afraid of them. The Lord your God, who is your leader, will himself fight for you, as he did in Egypt before all your eyes, and in the wilderness you yourselves saw it. The Lord your God carried you, as a man is wont to carry his little son, all along the way that you walked, until you came to this place. And even so you did not believe the Lord your God, who went before you on the way and marked out the place where you should pitch your tents, showing you by night the way by fire, and by day by a pillar of cloud. And when the Lord heard the sound of your words, he was angry and swore, saying: Not one of the men of this evil generation shall see the good land which I swore to give to your fathers, except Caleb the son of Jephunneh. He indeed shall see it, and to him and to his children will I give the land which he has trodden,

because he has followed the Lord. Nor is it a wonder that there was indignation against the people, since the Lord was angry with me also because of you, and said: You also shall not enter there; but Joshua the son of Nun, your minister, he shall enter in your place. Encourage and strengthen him, for he shall divide the land by lot to this people. Your little ones, of whom you said they would be led captive, and your children, who today do not know good from evil, they shall enter in, and to them I will give the land, and they shall possess it. But you, turn back and depart into the wilderness by way of the Red Sea. And you answered me: We have sinned against the Lord. We will go up and fight, as the Lord our God commanded us. And when, armed, you were preparing to go up into the mountain, the Lord said to me: Say to them: Do not go up nor fight, for I am not with you, lest you fall before your enemies. I spoke, and you did not listen; but resisting the Lord's command and swelling with pride, you went up into the mountain. So the Amorite who dwelt in the mountains came out and, meeting you, pursued you as bees are wont to pursue, and struck you down from Seir as far as Hormah. And when you returned and wept before the Lord, he did not hear you, nor would he listen to your voice. So you remained in Kadesh-barnea a long time. Chapter II.

And setting out from there, we came into the wilderness that leads to the Red Sea, as the Lord had told me, and we went about Mount Seir for a long time. And the Lord said to me: You have gone about this mountain long enough; turn toward the north, and command the people, saying: You shall pass through the borders of your brothers, the children of Esau, who dwell in Seir, and they will fear you. Take heed, therefore, diligently, that you do not stir up against them, for I will not give you from their land so much as a footstep of one foot;

